2026-06-30 15:18:38,731 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-042c56ea-b403-4de5-953b-c99880f69fd6', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nSeq68488_QE3.raw\nSeq69084_QE2.raw\nSeq69571_QE3.raw\nSeq69572_QE3.raw\nSeq69573_QE3.raw\nSeq69574_QE3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nNovel recognition site for polyUb and UBL signals in Rpn1\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nRpn1 is the largest proteasomal receptor for polyubiquitin and shuttle proteins contains one known binding site for polyubiquitin and shuttle proteins. We used a combination of NMR spectroscopy, photo-crosslinking, mass spectrometry, and mutagenesis,to confirm the presence of additional ubiquitin recognition site on RPN1. This novel site is situated in the N-terminal section of Rpn1, a region previously surmised to be devoid of functionality.\n\n[METHODS]\nS. cerevisiae Rpn1 fragment consists of residues 214-355  was expressed as a His6-Smt3-Rpn1 (214-355) fusion construct in E. coli BL21(DE3) Codon Plus cells. Following IPTG induction the cells were collected and the pellet was resuspended in 40 mL of Extraction Buffer (50 mM Tris, 500 mM potassium chloride, 20 mM imidazole, 7 M urea, 3 mM TCEP, pH 8.0). As before, DNAse I and one protease inhibitor tablet were added to the solution, which was then rocked overnight.The resulting solution was clarified by ultracentrifugation and filtered. The supernatant was then loaded onto a 5 mL HisTrap (GE Healthcare) column pre-equilibrated with Extraction Buffer. A shallow gradient with 50 mM Tris, 500 mM potassium chloride, 20 mM imidazole, 3 mM TCEP, pH 8.0 was used to slowly remove urea from the buffer over several hours, thereby allowing the protein to refold on the column. His6-Smt3-Rpn1214-355 was eluted from the column with 50 mM Tris, 500 mM potassium chloride, 250 mM imidazole, 3 mM TCEP, pH 8.0. His6-ULP1 was added to the solution to cleave His6-Smt3 from Rpn1214-355, and the solution was dialyzed overnight against 50 mM HEPES, 500 mM potassium chloride, 3 mM TCEP, 5% glycerol, pH 7.6. The solution was then loaded onto a pre-equilibrated 5 mL HisTrap column and Rpn1214-355 was collected in the flow-through. Gel filtration was used to separate monomeric Rpn1 (214-355) from any oligomeric species, whereby the solution was loaded onto a HiLoad 16/60 Superdex 75 pg (GE Healthcare) column equilibrated with 50 mM HEPES, 500 mM potassium chloride, 3 mM TCEP, pH 7.6.  Bpa-containing Ub monomers and dimers (Ub-T9Bpa or DiUb-Q49Bpa)from H. sapiens were generated and purified as described before(PMID: 30668904). Photo-crosslinking samples were prepared in 50 mM HEPES, 50 mM potassium chloride, 1 mM TCEP, pH 7.6 and contained 50 μM of Rpn1(214-355) (or Rpn1(214-324) or Rpn1(214-290)) and 25 μM of the Bpa-containing Ub or DiUbiquitin species. Samples were incubated on ice and exposed to UV irradiation at λ=365 nm (UV365nm) for one hour, as detailed elsewhere (PMID: 30668904). Crosslinking products of Rpn1(214-355) and Ub-T9Bpa were precipitated with 80% acetone and the ensuing pellet was resuspended in either 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate, 6M urea or 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate, 8M GuHCl. Cysteines were reduced with 5 mM DTT for 30 minutes and alkylated with 15 mM iodoacetamide for 30 minutes in the dark at room temperature. The samples were diluted with 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate to reduce the concentration of urea or GuHCl below 1 M, prior to digestion with sequencing grade trypsin (Promega) at a 1:100 w/w enzyme:substrate ratio for 12 hours at 37°C. Samples were lyophilized, dissolved in 8M GuHCl, and then subjected to a second digestion with trypsin (1:50 w/w). Crosslinking products of Rpn1(214-324) and Ub-T9Bpa or DiUb-Q49Bpa were prepared in a similar manner following in-gel digestion protocols described previously (PMID: 21048196).  The resulting peptide mixtures were desalted using C18 Stage-tips and subjected to LC-MS analysis using a Q Exactive Plus Orbitrap mass spectrometer coupled to nano-HPLC. The peptides were resolved by reversed-phase chromatography on 0.075\u202f×\u202f180\u202fmm fused silica capillaries (Agilent J&W) packed with Reprosil reversed-phase material (Dr. Maisch GmbH, Germany). The peptides were eluted with a linear 60\u202fminute gradient of 5–28% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid, followed by a 15\u202fminute gradient of 28–95% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid, and a 10\u202fminute wash of 95% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid (at flow rates of 0.15\u202fμl/min). MS was performed in positive mode using an m/z range of 300–1800, a resolution of 60,000 for MS1, and a resolution of 15,000 for MS2; repetitively full MS scans were followed by high energy collisional dissociation (HCD) of the 10 most dominant ions selected from the first MS scan.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae (baker\'s yeast); Homo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
