(K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nNovel recognition site for polyUb and UBL signals in Rpn1\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nRpn1 is the largest proteasomal receptor for polyubiquitin and shuttle proteins contains one known binding site for polyubiquitin and shuttle proteins. We used a combination of NMR spectroscopy, photo-crosslinking, mass spectrometry, and mutagenesis,to confirm the presence of additional ubiquitin recognition site on RPN1. This novel site is situated in the N-terminal section of Rpn1, a region previously surmised to be devoid of functionality.\n\n[METHODS]\nS. cerevisiae Rpn1 fragment consists of residues 214-355  was expressed as a His6-Smt3-Rpn1 (214-355) fusion construct in E. coli BL21(DE3) Codon Plus cells. Following IPTG induction the cells were collected and the pellet was resuspended in 40 mL of Extraction Buffer (50 mM Tris, 500 mM potassium chloride, 20 mM imidazole, 7 M urea, 3 mM TCEP, pH 8.0). As before, DNAse I and one protease inhibitor tablet were added to the solution, which was then rocked overnight.The resulting solution was clarified by ultracentrifugation and filtered. The supernatant was then loaded onto a 5 mL HisTrap (GE Healthcare) column pre-equilibrated with Extraction Buffer. A shallow gradient with 50 mM Tris, 500 mM potassium chloride, 20 mM imidazole, 3 mM TCEP, pH 8.0 was used to slowly remove urea from the buffer over several hours, thereby allowing the protein to refold on the column. His6-Smt3-Rpn1214-355 was eluted from the column with 50 mM Tris, 500 mM potassium chloride, 250 mM imidazole, 3 mM TCEP, pH 8.0. His6-ULP1 was added to the solution to cleave His6-Smt3 from Rpn1214-355, and the solution was dialyzed overnight against 50 mM HEPES, 500 mM potassium chloride, 3 mM TCEP, 5% glycerol, pH 7.6. The solution was then loaded onto a pre-equilibrated 5 mL HisTrap column and Rpn1214-355 was collected in the flow-through. Gel filtration was used to separate monomeric Rpn1 (214-355) from any oligomeric species, whereby the solution was loaded onto a HiLoad 16/60 Superdex 75 pg (GE Healthcare) column equilibrated with 50 mM HEPES, 500 mM potassium chloride, 3 mM TCEP, pH 7.6.  Bpa-containing Ub monomers and dimers (Ub-T9Bpa or DiUb-Q49Bpa)from H. sapiens were generated and purified as described before(PMID: 30668904). Photo-crosslinking samples were prepared in 50 mM HEPES, 50 mM potassium chloride, 1 mM TCEP, pH 7.6 and contained 50 μM of Rpn1(214-355) (or Rpn1(214-324) or Rpn1(214-290)) and 25 μM of the Bpa-containing Ub or DiUbiquitin species. Samples were incubated on ice and exposed to UV irradiation at λ=365 nm (UV365nm) for one hour, as detailed elsewhere (PMID: 30668904). Crosslinking products of Rpn1(214-355) and Ub-T9Bpa were precipitated with 80% acetone and the ensuing pellet was resuspended in either 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate, 6M urea or 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate, 8M GuHCl. Cysteines were reduced with 5 mM DTT for 30 minutes and alkylated with 15 mM iodoacetamide for 30 minutes in the dark at room temperature. The samples were diluted with 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate to reduce the concentration of urea or GuHCl below 1 M, prior to digestion with sequencing grade trypsin (Promega) at a 1:100 w/w enzyme:substrate ratio for 12 hours at 37°C. Samples were lyophilized, dissolved in 8M GuHCl, and then subjected to a second digestion with trypsin (1:50 w/w). Crosslinking products of Rpn1(214-324) and Ub-T9Bpa or DiUb-Q49Bpa were prepared in a similar manner following in-gel digestion protocols described previously (PMID: 21048196).  The resulting peptide mixtures were desalted using C18 Stage-tips and subjected to LC-MS analysis using a Q Exactive Plus Orbitrap mass spectrometer coupled to nano-HPLC. The peptides were resolved by reversed-phase chromatography on 0.075\u202f×\u202f180\u202fmm fused silica capillaries (Agilent J&W) packed with Reprosil reversed-phase material (Dr. Maisch GmbH, Germany). The peptides were eluted with a linear 60\u202fminute gradient of 5–28% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid, followed by a 15\u202fminute gradient of 28–95% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid, and a 10\u202fminute wash of 95% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid (at flow rates of 0.15\u202fμl/min). MS was performed in positive mode using an m/z range of 300–1800, a resolution of 60,000 for MS1, and a resolution of 15,000 for MS2; repetitively full MS scans were followed by high energy collisional dissociation (HCD) of the 10 most dominant ions selected from the first MS scan.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
